# Runtime output of the ingest redaction filter (redact.append_review_log).
# Holds kinds, offsets and fingerprints -- never the credential value, verified -- but it
# is per-deployment operational data, not source, and it grows. Operators back it up with
# the carts; it does not belong in the repo.
redaction-review.jsonl

# Journal and sidecar artifacts left by test runs against a cart in the tree.
*.journal.jsonl
*.journal.jsonl.hwm

# The journal writer's advisory lock. Transient, recreated on every append, and the one
# sidecar the 2026-11-02 sweep found untracked AND unignored -- which is the `git add -A`
# footgun, in a repo that genuinely pushes. Verified never committed (absent from origin/main
# and from all history) before adding this, so there is nothing to purge.
*.journal.jsonl.lock

# Generated render artifacts from the app-extraction tooling. Not source.
tools/test_output/

# ============================================================
# DATED BACKUPS -- our own convention, which defeated every rule above
# ============================================================
# Measured 2026-11-18. Every ignore rule in this file is anchored to an
# extension, and appending our standard `.bak-YYYY-MM-DD` suffix moves the
# filename past that anchor, so the backup of an ignored file is NOT ignored:
#
#   cartridges/x.cart.npz                 IGNORED   (cartridges/*.npz)
#   cartridges/x.cart.npz.bak-2026-11-18  NOT IGNORED
#   membot.log                            IGNORED   (*.log)
#   membot.log.bak-2026-11-18             NOT IGNORED
#
# ⛔ So the safer a backup made us feel, the more it exposed: a 465 KB cart plus
#   its notes sidecar would have landed untracked-and-unignored in a repo whose
#   client remote has push enabled. The .env family escaped only because
#   `.env.*` happens to be a prefix rule rather than a suffix one.
#
# ⓘ Verified before adding: zero `.bak` files tracked in this repo and none in
#   history, so there is nothing to purge. Studio was never exposed because it
#   ignores `cartridges/*` wholesale rather than by extension.
*.bak
*.bak-*

# ============================================================
# LOCAL-ONLY SAFETY AND HISTORY ARTIFACTS
# ============================================================
# ⚠ Glob the date, not the specific folder. The 2026-11-13 manifest upgrade left
#   two of these behind; hardcoding those two names would ignore exactly the ones
#   that already exist and none of the ones a future upgrade makes.
#
# A manifest backup is a local safety copy of a cart's integrity fingerprint. It
# must never ship: a second manifest for the same cart is a second answer to
# "what should this file hash to," and the whole point of the seal is that there
# is one.
manifest-backup*/

# A snapshot of what was running on the droplet, kept for comparison. Contains
# its own copy of membot_server.py, which is a deployment artifact and reads as
# source to anyone who clones.
droplet-snapshot-*/

# ============================================================
# BUILD ARTIFACTS THAT ARRIVE BY MERGE
# ============================================================
# ⭐ Added 2026-11-18 BEFORE reconciling with the fleet, not after. `fleet/main`
#   carries `.next/trace` (818 bytes) and `.next/trace-build` (214 bytes) as
#   tracked files, and we had no `.next` rule at all -- so a merge would have
#   silently adopted another repo's build output as our source.
#
# ⚠ The general point, which is the reason this block exists rather than two
#   lines: an ignore file protects you from what YOU create, and says nothing
#   about what a merge brings in. Before taking anyone else's history, check what
#   is tracked on their side that is merely untracked on ours.
.next/
*.tsbuildinfo
